    "growth_rate_source": "judgment: long-run nearline exabyte demand net of areal-density price deflation; no historical CAGR is like-for-like",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (-6.8%, FY2023-FY2026) diverges from revenue CAGR (+27.4%) by >30pp: equity was cut by the SanDisk spin-off and buybacks and then inflated by the SanDisk mark-to-market gains, so it is not a growth signal. Revenue 3yr CAGR runs trough (FY2023 HDD-only $6.3B, negative operating income) to peak (FY2026 $12.9B), so it overstates through-cycle growth. EPS and owner-FCF CAGRs are undefined (negative FY2023/FY2024 base). 5yr CAGRs are null because FY2021-FY2022 include Flash.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC): FY2024 -1,076; FY2025 1,014; FY2026 3,307. SBC is falling as a share of revenue (2.8% FY2025 -> 1.6% FY2026), so the SBC adjustment is not eating the growth; revenue and pricing are doing it."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "The AI-driven nearline shortage is real and contracted into 2028, so FY2027 grows ~28% at ~46% pre-SBC EBITDA (the Q4 FY2026 exit rate). The HDD industry has never sustained peak pricing, though: Toshiba is doubling AI-datacenter capacity by its fiscal 2027, and the HAMR ramps at both WDC and Seagate add exabytes per unit of capex. The base therefore has a normal down-leg in FY2029-FY2030 (revenue -12% peak to trough) and settles at a 32% mid-cycle margin, above anything this industry earned before 2025 because the market is now two players plus a distant third. Exit at 13x owner FCF.",
    "bull": "Management is right that 'disciplined capacity' has ended boom-bust. Nearline exabyte demand keeps growing 40-50%/yr against ~30% supply growth into 2029, multi-year hyperscaler agreements lock in pricing, and HAMR (100TB by 2029) keeps cost per TB falling faster than price, so margins stay near 40% and revenue roughly triples over the decade. The market then pays a quasi-non-cyclical 16x for the cash flows.",
    "bear": "Classic storage-cycle reversal. AI capex digestion in 2027 coincides with Toshiba's new Philippine capacity and Seagate's HAMR yield improvements; the three hyperscalers that are ~45% of revenue push back on pricing, and the single-product, post-spin WDC has no flash business to offset. Revenue falls ~30% from the FY2027 peak and margins revert to ~22% (still better than the FY2023-FY2024 losses). Exit at 10x."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Mid-cycle owner-FCF valuation of a pure-play HDD duopolist at a cyclical peak. GAAP earnings are ignored because the SanDisk mark-to-market gains dominate them. The flows start from operating EBITDA, carry the upcycle through FY2027-FY2028, and normalize to a mid-cycle margin. Cyclicality is priced in the scenarios and weights, not the discount rate.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Mid-cycle margin judgment: each 1pp of terminal pre-SBC EBITDA margin moves base value by roughly 3-4%; 32% is above all pre-2025 HDD history",
      "Q1 FY2027 guidance (August 2026) not in the research corpus; year-1 path is extrapolated from the Q4 FY2026 exit rate",
      "Convertible notes ($1,060M, deep in the money): the diluted count may understate shares by a few million if principal is equity-settled",
      "Equity-award withholding taxes are undisclosed in XBRL and approximated by lifting forward SBC to 2.0% of revenue",
      "Customer concentration: three hyperscalers are ~45% of revenue"
    ]
  },
